  - [1m[38;5;153mtask_event[0m — id, [38;5;153mtask_id[39m, [38;5;153mactor_id[39m, [38;5;153mkind[39m, [38;5;153mpayload[39m (JSON), created_at. Kinds: created, assigned, unassigned,
    status_changed, title_changed, description_changed, due_date_changed. The payload holds before/after values, so the
    inbox can say "moved from In Progress to Done" without re-reading the task. Indexed on [38;5;153m(task_id, created_at)[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153msubscription[0m — [38;5;153muser_id[39m, [38;5;153mtask_id[39m, [38;5;153msource[39m (auto_assignee / auto_creator / manual), [38;5;153mmuted[39m, created_at. Unique on
    [38;5;153m(user_id, task_id)[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153mnotification[0m — id, [38;5;153muser_id[39m, [38;5;153mtask_event_id[39m, nullable [38;5;153mread_at[39m, created_at. Unique on [38;5;153m(user_id, task_event_id)[39m. Indexed
    on [38;5;153m(user_id, read_at, created_at DESC)[39m for the inbox query and the unread badge.

  Four decisions worth stating outright, because each one could reasonably have gone the other way:

  [1mA notification points at its event; it doesn't copy it.[0m One source of truth for what happened, and much smaller writes
  when a task has many watchers. The cost is a join on the inbox query and a renderer that must tolerate old payload
  shapes once event kinds evolve.

  [1m[38;5;153mmuted[39m is a column, not a deleted row.[0m Deleting the subscription would mean "unfollow" silently undoes itself the next
  time someone reassigns the task to you — the auto-subscribe rule would just re-create it. The flag distinguishes [3mnever
[0m  [3msubscribed[0m from [3mdeliberately left[0m.

  [1mNobody is notified of their own action.[0m Fan-out skips the event's [38;5;153mactor_id[39m. This is the single most common complaint
  about notification systems, and it's one [38;5;153mWHERE[39m clause here versus a retrofit later.

  [1mUnique [38;5;153m(user_id, task_event_id)[39m makes fan-out idempotent.[0m A retried or double-run fan-out inserts nothing the second
  time. That's what lets the post-commit fan-out fail safely, which is the subject of the next section.
